//! crypto/kumara_identity.rye — Season G (Cryptography), the identity arc's FRONT DOOR: the one composition that ties the whole arc into a single
//! answer — from a backup phrase to a keeper's Kumara signing identity, and the signature it makes. Where the rungs beneath authored each piece
//! (crypto/bip39_seed folds a mnemonic to a 512-bit seed, crypto/slip10_ed25519 grows that seed into an ed25519 HD tree, crypto/kumara_path names
//! the copyable shape m/44'/coin_type'/account'/index' of a keeper's identity within it, crypto/ed25519_sign makes and derives the signature), this
//! rung COMPOSES them so a caller reaches identity through one door: phrase → seed → path → keypair → signature. It is the identity-arc sibling of
//! the wallet arc's crypto/eth_address.rye and crypto/bitcoin_address.rye — where those render a key to its human address, this renders a phrase to
//! the identity every Grain module signs and verifies with (Kumara, Vault, Comlink, the Lotus signed carry).
//!
//! It authors NO new cryptography. Every HMAC, clamp, scalar, and signature lives in an already-GREEN rung beneath; this module contributes only
//! the wiring — the four lines that turn a phrase into an identity, and the two thin wrappers (sign / verify) that let a caller sign with the
//! derived key without reaching past this door. A Kumara identity's public key is the raw 32-byte ed25519 public key (kumara/README.md: "an
//! Ed25519 keypair, deterministic from a 32-byte seed"), which is exactly crypto/slip10_ed25519.rye's 33-byte public form with its 0x00 SLIP-0010
//! domain tag stripped — and, independently, exactly crypto/ed25519_sign.rye's derive_public of the node's key. This module asserts those two agree.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md — Season G, "the library that serves Kumara
//! identity, Vault, Comlink, and the Lotus signed carry." The 1,024-round itinerary, Season 2 Journey 6 (Provenance): "a real document carries a
//! Kumara-signed, content-addressed chain of custody end to end." This rung stands the identity end of that chain: the key a Kumara signature is
//! made with, reached the same way from the same phrase on any device.
//! Kin: crypto/kumara_path.rye (the account path this walks, GREEN) · crypto/bip39_seed.rye (the mnemonic→seed fold, GREEN) ·
//! crypto/slip10_ed25519.rye (the ed25519 HD tree, GREEN) · crypto/ed25519_sign.rye + crypto/ed25519_verify.rye (the signature, GREEN) ·
//! crypto/signed_carry.rye (the composition that seals a record under such an identity's seed). Clean-room: the path convention, BIP-39, and
//! SLIP-0010's vectors are public standards studied through their specs (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md); no line is copied.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL, no network, no funds, no real device. Every identity derived here is a TEST identity from a
//! published test-vector mnemonic or seed; this module calls no std.crypto. The derived secret seed is a secret the caller keeps in their own jail,
//! exactly as Kumara's own keypair does; timing-safety of the signing and derivation it composes stays crypto/CONSTANT_TIME.md's named horizon, not
//! introduced here. Growing a REAL Kumara identity from the maintainer's OWN phrase, and signing a real record with it, stays the custody gate
//! (#3/#4) — this library derives and verifies a TEST identity; it never holds the maintainer's key.
//!
//! Parity is proven at selftest three replayable ways, no fabricated constant: (1) the bip39 half is anchored to a REAL published answer — the
//! intermediate seed of BIP-39's own Trezor vector mnemonic "abandon…about" under passphrase "TREZOR" equals the spec's published 512-bit seed
//! byte-for-byte; (2) the arc has no drift — from_mnemonic equals hand-wiring bip39_seed → kumara_path.derive_identity, and the identity's public
//! key equals both crypto/slip10_ed25519.rye's public form (0x00 tag stripped) and crypto/ed25519_sign.rye's independent derive_public, all three
//! agreeing; the ed25519 half itself is proven one rung down, where crypto/kumara_path.rye reproduces SLIP-0010's own ed25519 test vectors; (3) the
//! derived key is a REAL working signing key — it signs a message that verify accepts, a flipped message byte and a flipped signature byte are each
//! refused, and a DIFFERENT identity's public key refuses the same signature. Determinism (same phrase → identical identity) and distinctness
//! (a changed account, index, or coin → a different public key) close the proof.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run crypto/kumara_identity.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const bip39 = @import("bip39_seed.rye");
const kumara_path = @import("kumara_path.rye");
const slip10 = @import("slip10_ed25519.rye");
const hmac = @import("hmac_sha512.rye");
const signer = @import("ed25519_sign.rye");
const verifier = @import("ed25519_verify.rye");

pub const kumara_identity_version = "20260816.092122";

/// The 32-byte ed25519 secret seed a Kumara signature is made with. Held by the caller in their own jail, never by this library beyond a single
/// derivation; the same width Kumara's own keypair_from_seed takes.
pub const secret_len: u32 = slip10.key_len; // 32

/// The raw 32-byte ed25519 public key that names a Kumara identity — the SLIP-0010 33-byte form with its 0x00 domain tag stripped.
pub const public_len: u32 = slip10.key_len; // 32

/// The chain code carried at the identity node, so a caller can derive further children (a per-record subkey) without re-walking from the seed.
pub const chain_len: u32 = slip10.chain_code_len; // 32

/// One derived Kumara identity: the secret signing seed, the public key that names it, the chain code at its node, and the three path slots that
/// reached it. secret_seed is a secret; public_key, chain_code, and the slots are public.
pub const Identity = struct {
    secret_seed: [secret_len]u8,
    public_key: [public_len]u8,
    chain_code: [chain_len]u8,
    coin_type: u32,
    account: u32,
    index: u32,
};

/// Strip the SLIP-0010 33-byte public form (0x00 ‖ 32-byte key) to the raw 32-byte ed25519 public key a Kumara identity is named by. Asserts the
/// domain tag it removes is the 0x00 SLIP-0010 expects, so a wrong-shaped input is caught rather than silently truncated.
fn strip_domain_tag(slip_public: *const [slip10.public_len]u8) [public_len]u8 {
    // invariant: the SLIP-0010 public form always opens with the 0x00 ed25519 domain tag (crypto/slip10_ed25519.rye public_key).
    assert(slip_public[0] == 0x00);
    var out: [public_len]u8 = undefined;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < public_len) : (k += 1) {
        // invariant: the 32-byte key sits after exactly one tag byte, so index k reads slip_public[1 + k] within the 33-byte form.
        assert((1 + k) < slip10.public_len);
        out[k] = slip_public[1 + k];
    }
    return out;
}

/// Assemble the Identity from a walked node — shared by from_seed and from_mnemonic so the two paths cannot drift. The public key is derived two
/// independent ways and asserted equal: crypto/slip10_ed25519.rye's public form (tag stripped) and crypto/ed25519_sign.rye's derive_public. Both
/// run the same ed25519 public derivation over the same node key, so an inequality would mean a real inconsistency between the two rungs.
fn identity_from_node(node: *const slip10.Node, coin_type: u32, account: u32, index: u32) Identity {
    const slip_public = slip10.public_key(node);
    const public_key = strip_domain_tag(&slip_public);
    const direct = signer.derive_public(&node.key);
    // invariant: the two independent public derivations agree byte-for-byte — the identity's public key is unambiguous.
    var k: u32 = 0;
    // invariant: the derived key is compared byte for byte against the one computed directly,
    // so the two paths are proven to agree rather than assumed to
    while (k < public_len) : (k += 1) assert(public_key[k] == direct[k]);

    return Identity{
        .secret_seed = node.key,
        .public_key = public_key,
        .chain_code = node.chain_code,
        .coin_type = coin_type,
        .account = account,
        .index = index,
    };
}

/// Derive a Kumara identity directly from a BIP-39 seed (the 512-bit output of crypto/bip39_seed.rye, or any SLIP-0010-admissible byte string),
/// walking crypto/kumara_path.rye's m/44'/coin_type'/account'/index'. The mathematics is all one rung down; this is the wiring plus the two-way
/// public-key check. TEST seeds only — a real seed reaches the custody gate.
pub fn from_seed(
    seed: []const u8,
    coin_type: u32,
    account: u32,
    index: u32,
) (kumara_path.KumaraPathError || slip10.Slip10Error || hmac.HmacError)!Identity {
    const node = try kumara_path.derive_identity(seed, coin_type, account, index);
    // invariant: the walked node sits at the Kumara path's fixed depth of four below the master.
    assert(node.depth == 4);
    return identity_from_node(&node, coin_type, account, index);
}

/// The whole identity arc in one call: fold a mnemonic (and optional passphrase) to a 512-bit seed with crypto/bip39_seed.rye, then walk the
/// Kumara path to the identity. Composes bip39_seed and kumara_path entirely — no drift from calling from_seed on bip39_seed's output by hand,
/// which the selftest asserts. TEST mnemonics only — the maintainer's own phrase reaches the custody gate.
pub fn from_mnemonic(
    mnemonic: []const u8,
    passphrase: []const u8,
    coin_type: u32,
    account: u32,
    index: u32,
) (bip39.Bip39Error || kumara_path.KumaraPathError || slip10.Slip10Error)!Identity {
    var seed_buf: [bip39.seed_len]u8 = undefined;
    try bip39.seed(&seed_buf, mnemonic, passphrase);
    // from_seed over a fixed 64-byte seed cannot raise an HMAC message-bound error, yet the error union is carried honestly.
    return from_seed(seed_buf[0..], coin_type, account, index) catch |err| switch (err) {
        error.IndexTooLarge => error.IndexTooLarge,
        error.NotHardened => error.NotHardened,
        error.PathTooDeep => error.PathTooDeep,
        error.BadPath => error.BadPath,
        error.DepthTooDeep => error.DepthTooDeep,
        else => unreachable, // an HMAC bound cannot trip on a 64-byte seed
    };
}

/// Sign a message with a derived identity's secret seed — the thin wrapper that lets a caller make a Kumara signature through this one door rather
/// than reaching into crypto/ed25519_sign.rye. Reads the secret seed; the signature it returns is public.
pub fn sign(identity: *const Identity, msg: []const u8) signer.SignError![64]u8 {
    return signer.sign(&identity.secret_seed, msg);
}

/// Verify a signature against an identity's public key — the public counterpart of sign, so a verifier need hold only the 32-byte public key.
pub fn verify(public_key: *const [public_len]u8, msg: []const u8, sig: *const [64]u8) verifier.VerifyError!void {
    return verifier.verify(sig, msg, public_key);
}

// ---- selftest ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn nibble(c: u8) u8 {
    if (c >= '0' and c <= '9') return c - '0';
    // invariant: the vector hex uses only lowercase a–f beyond the digits.
    assert(c >= 'a' and c <= 'f');
    return c - 'a' + 10;
}

/// Compare a raw byte buffer against its lowercase-hex spelling — the replayable check the anchored seed faces.
fn eq_hex(raw: []const u8, hex: []const u8) bool {
    if (raw.len * 2 != hex.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < raw.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (raw[i] != nibble(hex[i * 2]) * 16 + nibble(hex[i * 2 + 1])) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

/// Compare two raw byte buffers.
fn bytes_eq(a: []const u8, b: []const u8) bool {
    if (a.len != b.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < a.len) : (i += 1) if (a[i] != b[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

// BIP-39's own published Trezor vector 1 — a REAL published answer anchoring the mnemonic→seed half of the arc.
const trezor_mnemonic = "abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon about";
const trezor_passphrase = "TREZOR";
const trezor_seed_hex = "c55257c360c07c72029aebc1b53c05ed0362ada38ead3e3e9efa3708e53495531f09a6987599d18264c1e1c92f2cf141630c7a3c4ab7c81b2f001698e7463b04";

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // A caller-supplied coin slot: a registered Grain SLIP-44 identity coin type is a named horizon, so the tests pass an explicit value rather
    // than fabricate a registry number (crypto/kumara_path.rye purpose note).
    const test_coin: u32 = 7;

    // 1. REAL-ANSWER ANCHOR (the bip39 half). Fold the published Trezor mnemonic to a seed the standard-published way, and assert it equals the
    //    spec's own 512-bit seed byte-for-byte — so the mnemonic→seed half of from_mnemonic stands on a real answer, not a claim.
    var anchored_seed: [bip39.seed_len]u8 = undefined;
    try bip39.seed(&anchored_seed, trezor_mnemonic, trezor_passphrase);
    if (!eq_hex(anchored_seed[0..], trezor_seed_hex)) return error.Bip39SeedAnchorMismatch;

    // 2. ARC HAS NO DRIFT. from_mnemonic must equal hand-wiring bip39_seed → kumara_path.derive_identity, and the identity's fields must match the
    //    walked node exactly. Any drift between the front door and the rungs beneath is caught here.
    const id = try from_mnemonic(trezor_mnemonic, trezor_passphrase, test_coin, 0, 0);
    const by_hand_node = try kumara_path.derive_identity(anchored_seed[0..], test_coin, 0, 0);
    if (!bytes_eq(id.secret_seed[0..], by_hand_node.key[0..])) return error.SecretDrift;
    if (!bytes_eq(id.chain_code[0..], by_hand_node.chain_code[0..])) return error.ChainDrift;
    // from_seed on the same seed reaches the identical identity — the two entry points agree.
    const id_by_seed = try from_seed(anchored_seed[0..], test_coin, 0, 0);
    if (!bytes_eq(id.secret_seed[0..], id_by_seed.secret_seed[0..])) return error.SeedEntryDrift;
    if (!bytes_eq(id.public_key[0..], id_by_seed.public_key[0..])) return error.SeedEntryPublicDrift;

    // 3. THREE-WAY PUBLIC-KEY AGREEMENT. The identity's public key equals crypto/slip10_ed25519.rye's public form (0x00 tag stripped) and
    //    crypto/ed25519_sign.rye's independent derive_public — the same 32 bytes three ways, with the SLIP form's domain tag proven 0x00.
    const slip_public = slip10.public_key(&by_hand_node);
    if (slip_public[0] != 0x00) return error.DomainTagWrong;
    if (!bytes_eq(id.public_key[0..], slip_public[1..])) return error.PublicVsSlipMismatch;
    const direct_public = signer.derive_public(&by_hand_node.key);
    if (!bytes_eq(id.public_key[0..], direct_public[0..])) return error.PublicVsDirectMismatch;

    // 4. THE DERIVED KEY IS A REAL WORKING SIGNING KEY. It signs a message that verify accepts; a flipped message byte, a flipped signature byte,
    //    and a DIFFERENT identity's public key each refuse — the whole point of an identity, proven, tying ed25519 sign/verify into the arc.
    const message = "Grain — a key becomes a name when someone keeps it, and signs so.";
    const sig = try sign(&id, message);
    try verify(&id.public_key, message, &sig);

    // A flipped message byte no longer verifies.
    {
        var tampered: [message.len]u8 = undefined;
        var k: u32 = 0;
        while (k < message.len) : (k += 1) tampered[k] = message[k];
        tampered[5] ^= 0x01;
        if (verify(&id.public_key, tampered[0..], &sig)) |_| return error.TamperMsgAccepted else |_| {}
    }
    // A flipped signature byte no longer verifies.
    {
        var bad_sig = sig;
        bad_sig[10] ^= 0x80;
        if (verify(&id.public_key, message, &bad_sig)) |_| return error.TamperSigAccepted else |_| {}
    }
    // A different identity's public key refuses this signature.
    {
        const other = try from_mnemonic(trezor_mnemonic, trezor_passphrase, test_coin, 1, 0);
        if (bytes_eq(id.public_key[0..], other.public_key[0..])) return error.SiblingCollision;
        if (verify(&other.public_key, message, &sig)) |_| return error.WrongKeyAccepted else |_| {}
    }

    // 5. DETERMINISM AND DISTINCTNESS. The same phrase and slots reach an identical identity; a changed account, index, or coin reaches a
    //    different public key — so an identity is stable per path and separated across paths.
    {
        const again = try from_mnemonic(trezor_mnemonic, trezor_passphrase, test_coin, 0, 0);
        if (!bytes_eq(id.public_key[0..], again.public_key[0..])) return error.NotDeterministic;
        if (!bytes_eq(id.secret_seed[0..], again.secret_seed[0..])) return error.SecretNotDeterministic;

        const other_account = try from_mnemonic(trezor_mnemonic, trezor_passphrase, test_coin, 2, 0);
        const other_index = try from_mnemonic(trezor_mnemonic, trezor_passphrase, test_coin, 0, 5);
        const other_coin = try from_mnemonic(trezor_mnemonic, trezor_passphrase, test_coin + 1, 0, 0);
        if (bytes_eq(id.public_key[0..], other_account.public_key[0..])) return error.AccountNotSeparated;
        if (bytes_eq(id.public_key[0..], other_index.public_key[0..])) return error.IndexNotSeparated;
        if (bytes_eq(id.public_key[0..], other_coin.public_key[0..])) return error.CoinNotSeparated;
    }

    // 6. EDGES. A level at or above the hardened offset (2³¹) collides with the hardening bit and is refused IndexTooLarge — the guard
    //    crypto/kumara_path.rye's derive_identity raises, reached through this door.
    {
        const at_offset: u32 = 1 << 31; // exactly the hardened offset — one past the largest legal bare value
        if (from_seed(anchored_seed[0..], at_offset, 0, 0)) |_| return error.OffsetCoinAccepted else |err| if (err != error.IndexTooLarge) return error.WrongOffsetCoin;
        if (from_seed(anchored_seed[0..], 0, at_offset, 0)) |_| return error.OffsetAccountAccepted else |err| if (err != error.IndexTooLarge) return error.WrongOffsetAccount;
        if (from_seed(anchored_seed[0..], 0, 0, at_offset)) |_| return error.OffsetIndexAccepted else |err| if (err != error.IndexTooLarge) return error.WrongOffsetIndex;
    }

    print("GREEN crypto-kumara-identity: the identity arc's FRONT DOOR authored in pure Rye — the one composition that ties phrase → seed → path " ++
        "→ keypair → signature into a single answer, the identity every Grain module signs and verifies with (Kumara, Vault, Comlink, the Lotus " ++
        "signed carry). The identity-arc sibling of crypto/eth_address and crypto/bitcoin_address: where those render a key to its human address, " ++
        "this renders a backup phrase to the Kumara signing identity. Authors NO new cryptography — from_mnemonic folds a mnemonic to a 512-bit " ++
        "seed through the GREEN crypto/bip39_seed, walks crypto/kumara_path's m/44'/coin'/account'/index' over the GREEN crypto/slip10_ed25519 HD " ++
        "tree, and reads off the raw 32-byte ed25519 public key (Kumara's identity form); sign / verify are thin wrappers over crypto/ed25519_sign " ++
        "+ crypto/ed25519_verify so a caller reaches identity through one door. Proven GREEN on metal three replayable ways with no fabricated " ++
        "constant: (1) the bip39 half anchored to a REAL published answer — the Trezor vector mnemonic 'abandon…about' under 'TREZOR' folds to the " ++
        "spec's own 512-bit seed byte-for-byte; (2) the arc has no drift — from_mnemonic equals hand-wiring bip39_seed → kumara_path.derive_identity, " ++
        "from_seed reaches the identical identity, and the public key equals both slip10's public form (0x00 tag stripped) and ed25519_sign's " ++
        "independent derive_public, all three agreeing (the ed25519 half proven one rung down against SLIP-0010's own ed25519 vectors); (3) the " ++
        "derived key is a REAL working signing key — it signs a message verify accepts, while a flipped message byte, a flipped signature byte, and " ++
        "a different identity's public key each refuse. Determinism (same phrase → identical identity) and distinctness (a changed account, index, " ++
        "or coin → a different public key), plus the hardened-offset edge each raising IndexTooLarge, close the proof. Purely local — TEST " ++
        "identities from published vectors, no real identity key, no network, no funds, no real device; growing a REAL Kumara identity from the " ++
        "maintainer's own phrase and signing a real record with it stays the custody gate. With this rung the identity arc stands whole and " ++
        "reachable through one door: phrase → seed → ed25519 HD tree → Kumara identity path → the signing identity itself.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
